1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,122
Before the live proof, here's what's under the hood.

2
00:00:03,122 --> 00:00:11,289
Two independent Praxis AI gateways sit in front of three applications: app-a, app-b, and app-c, each governed by a per-app token budget.

3
00:00:11,289 --> 00:00:13,330
Every request carries two headers.

4
00:00:13,330 --> 00:00:18,914
X-tier selects one of two rules: gold-tier, a sliding window, or silver-tier, a token bucket.

5
00:00:18,914 --> 00:00:23,358
X-app-id then selects that app's own budget within whichever rule matched.

6
00:00:23,358 --> 00:00:33,625
Both rules reserve tokens against the same shared Valkey ledger, so the budget is shared across every gateway replica, not local to whichever instance handled the request.

7
00:00:33,625 --> 00:00:48,337
Watch a few example requests move through this path: matched to a rule, reserved against the shared ledger, and only then forwarded to the backend, or stopped cold, before ever reaching a backend, if the ledger says that budget is already spent.

8
00:00:48,337 --> 00:00:59,538
This demo proves per app token budgets are enforced consistently across gateway replicas, for two independently chosen rate limiting algorithms, and that each recovers on its own.

9
00:00:59,538 --> 00:01:03,669
Two independent Praxis AI gateways share one Valkey backed ledger.

10
00:01:03,669 --> 00:01:11,553
App-a and app-c are matched to the gold tier rule, a sliding window; app-b is matched to the silver tier rule, a token bucket.

11
00:01:11,553 --> 00:01:14,933
App-a's first request, through Gateway A, is admitted.

12
00:01:14,933 --> 00:01:22,004
Its next request, through Gateway B, a separate process, is denied: both gateways consult the same shared ledger.

13
00:01:22,004 --> 00:01:27,010
App-c, on the same sliding window rule, is admitted on its own untouched budget.

14
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:38,712
Now app-b, on the token bucket rule, is admitted through Gateway A, and likewise denied through Gateway B: the shared ledger guarantee holds for token bucket too, not just sliding window.

15
00:01:38,712 --> 00:01:41,653
Now watch each budget recover on its own terms.

16
00:01:41,653 --> 00:01:48,662
App-a's window slides forward, its earlier reservation ages out, and it is admitted again, with no manual reset.

17
00:01:48,662 --> 00:01:57,548
App-b's bucket, meanwhile, refills continuously, token by token, and is also admitted again, typically sooner than a fixed window would allow.

18
00:01:57,548 --> 00:02:05,809
One shared ledger, two algorithms chosen per rule, isolated per app budgets, and automatic recovery, across a real process boundary.

